{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /work/answer.md'","aggregated_output":"# Exact condition\n\nWrite (V=v_A+v_B+v_C) and (m=\\min(v_A,v_B,v_C)), with the usual nonnegative valuations and (v_P<V). If all three winner values are positive, then\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\quad p^{\\mathrm{VN}}=\\operatorname{centroid}(F)\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nv_P\\le m\\ \\text{ or }\\ v_P\\ge V-m\\ \\text{ or }\\ v_A=v_B=v_C.\\quad}\n\\]\n\nThus, if the winner values are unequal, equality holds exactly when the package value is at most the smallest winner value, or at least the sum of the two largest winner values. The endpoints are included.\n\nIf any winner value is zero, equality always holds: the face is a segment or a point, and the Vickrey-nearest point is its centroid. Here and below, a lower-dimensional face has its centroid with respect to its own dimension.\n\n## Proof\n\nFirst suppose all winner values and (v_P) are positive. Set\n\n\\[\nd=V-v_P>0,\\qquad\n\\ell_i=(v_i-d)_+=p_i^{\\mathrm{VCG}},\\qquad\nw_i=v_i-\\ell_i=\\min(v_i,d),\\qquad\nr=v_P-\\sum_i\\ell_i=\\sum_iw_i-d.\n\\]\n\n**Reduction to a capped simplex.** The core constraint for all three winners requires total payments at least (v_P). Conversely, every vector satisfying\n\n\\[\n0\\le p_i\\le v_i,\\qquad \\sum_i p_i=v_P\n\\]\n\nis in the core: for any subset (T),\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{i\\in T}p_i\n=v_P-\\sum_{j\\notin T}p_j\n\\ge v_P-\\sum_{j\\notin T}v_j,\n\\]\n\nand its payments are nonnegative. Such vectors exist because (0<v_P<V). Consequently, the minimum revenue is (v_P), and these vectors constitute (F).\n\nThe singleton constraints imply (p_i\\ge\\ell_i). Translating by (\\ell) therefore identifies (F) with\n\n\\[\nK=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^3:0\\le x_i\\le w_i,\\quad x_A+x_B+x_C=r\\}.\n\\]\n\nTranslation preserves centroids, and the Vickrey-nearest point becomes the point of (K) minimizing (\\sum_i x_i^2). Here all (w_i>0) and (0<r<\\sum_iw_i), so (K) has positive area in its plane.\n\n**A centroid fact.** For positive caps (w_i) and (0<r<\\sum_iw_i), the centroid (c) of (K) has equal coordinates if and only if\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\quad r\\le\\min_i w_i\\quad\\text{or}\\quad w_A=w_B=w_C.\\quad} \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nSufficiency follows from symmetry: in the first case all upper bounds are redundant, giving an equilateral triangle; in the second case (K) is invariant under every coordinate permutation.\n\nFor necessity, suppose (w_i<w_j). Slice (K) at a fixed value of the remaining coordinate (x_k=t), and put (s=r-t\\). The feasible interval for (x_i) is\n\n\\[\n\\max(0,s-w_j)\\le x_i\\le\\min(w_i,s).\n\\]\n\nThe mean of (x_j-x_i) along this segment is\n\n\\[\ns-\\max(0,s-w_j)-\\min(w_i,s)\n=\\min(s,w_j)-\\min(s,w_i)\\ge0.\n\\]\n\nIf (r>w_i), it is strictly positive on a positive-area collection of slices. Indeed, choose\n\n\\[\n\\max(0,r-w_i-w_j)<t<\\min(w_k,r-w_i).\n\\]\n\nThis interval is nonempty because (r>w_i) and (r<\\sum_iw_i). These slices have positive length and (s>w_i). Integrating the slice means (weighted by their lengths) gives (c_j>c_i). Thus, if the caps are unequal and (r>\\min_iw_i), choose a smallest cap and a larger cap to see that the centroid coordinates cannot all be equal. This proves (1).\n\n**When the centroid minimizes distance.** The centroid is in the relative interior of (K). If it minimizes (\\sum_i x_i^2), differentiation along each direction (e_i-e_j) gives (c_i=c_j). Conversely, if its coordinates are equal, it is ((r/3,r/3,r/3)), the unique minimum of that squared norm on the entire plane \\(\\sum_i x_i=r\\). Hence (1) is precisely the condition for the two points to agree. When they agree, their payment vector is\n\n\\[\np_i=\\ell_i+\\frac r3.\n\\]\n\n**Translate the condition back to values.** Sort the winner values as (a\\le b\\le c). Their effective caps are ordered in the same way. Since (r=\\sum_iw_i-d),\n\n\\[\nr\\le\\min_iw_i\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\min(b,d)+\\min(c,d)\\le d\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nb+c\\le d\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nv_P\\le a.\n\\]\n\nFor the middle equivalence, if (c\\ge d), the left side is strictly greater than (d) because (b>0); otherwise both caps equal their original values. Also,\n\n\\[\n\\min(a,d)=\\min(b,d)=\\min(c,d)\n\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\nd\\le a\\ \\text{ or }\\ a=b=c.\n\\]\n\nThe condition (d\\le a) is exactly (v_P\\ge b+c=V-m). These are the three alternatives in the theorem.\n\nFinally, (v_P=0) gives (F=\\{0\\}). If a winner value is zero and (v_P>0), omit its fixed zero coordinate. In the same translation, at most two caps (w_1,w_2\\le d) remain and (r=w_1+w_2-d\\). Thus (r\\le w_1,w_2), so all upper bounds are redundant: the translated face is the segment from ((r,0)) to ((0,r)), or a point if (r=0). Its minimum-norm point is its midpoint, proving the zero-value assertion.\n\n## Worked example where they differ\n\nTake\n\n\\[\n(v_P,v_A,v_B,v_C)=(2,1,2,2).\n\\]\n\nHere (V=5), (m=1), and neither (2\\le1) nor (2\\ge4) holds; the winner values are unequal. VCG charges zero to every winner, and\n\n\\[\nF=\\{p_A+p_B+p_C=2,\\quad 0\\le p_A\\le1,\\quad p_B,p_C\\ge0\\}.\n\\]\n\n(The caps (p_B,p_C\\le2) follow from these constraints.) The equal-payment point is feasible and minimizes the squared distance to zero, so\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{p^{\\mathrm{VN}}=(2/3,2/3,2/3).}\n\\]\n\nTo compute the area centroid, start with the triangle having vertices ((2,0,0),(0,2,0),(0,0,2)), whose centroid is ((2/3,2/3,2/3)). Remove the triangle having vertices ((2,0,0),(1,1,0),(1,0,1)), whose centroid is ((4/3,1/3,1/3)). The removed triangle has one quarter of the original area. Therefore\n\n\\[\n\\operatorname{centroid}(F)\n=\\frac{4(2/3,2/3,2/3)-(4/3,1/3,1/3)}3\n=\\boxed{(4/9,7/9,7/9)},\n\\]\n\nwhich differs from the Vickrey-nearest point.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /work/answer.md <<'EOF'\n# Exact condition\n\nWrite \\\\(V=v_A+v_B+v_C\\\\) and \\\\(m=\\\\min(v_A,v_B,v_C)\\\\), with the usual nonnegative valuations and \\\\(v_P<V\\\\). If all three winner values are positive, then\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{\\\\quad p\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VN}}=\\\\operatorname{centroid}(F)\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\nv_P\\\\le m\\\\ \\\\text{ or }\\\\ v_P\\\\ge V-m\\\\ \\\\text{ or }\\\\ v_A=v_B=v_C.\\\\quad}\n\\\\]\n\nThus, if the winner values are unequal, equality holds exactly when the package value is at most the smallest winner value, or at least the sum of the two largest winner values. The endpoints are included.\n\nIf any winner value is zero, equality always holds: the face is a segment or a point, and the Vickrey-nearest point is its centroid. Here and below, a lower-dimensional face has its centroid with respect to its own dimension.\n\n## Proof\n\nFirst suppose all winner values and \\\\(v_P\\\\) are positive. Set\n\n\\\\[\nd=V-v_P>0,\\\\qquad\n\\\\ell_i=(v_i-d)_+=p_i\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VCG}},\\\\qquad\nw_i=v_i-\\\\ell_i=\\\\min(v_i,d),\\\\qquad\nr=v_P-\\\\sum_i\\\\ell_i=\\\\sum_iw_i-d.\n\\\\]\n\n**Reduction to a capped simplex.** The core constraint for all three winners requires total payments at least \\\\(v_P\\\\). Conversely, every vector satisfying\n\n\\\\[\n0\\\\le p_i\\\\le v_i,\\\\qquad \\\\sum_i p_i=v_P\n\\\\]\n\nis in the core: for any subset \\\\(T\\\\),\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\sum_{i\\\\in T}p_i\n=v_P-\\\\sum_{j\\\\notin T}p_j\n\\\\ge v_P-\\\\sum_{j\\\\notin T}v_j,\n\\\\]\n\nand its payments are nonnegative. Such vectors exist because \\\\(0<v_P<V\\\\). Consequently, the minimum revenue is \\\\(v_P\\\\), and these vectors constitute \\\\(F\\\\).\n\nThe singleton constraints imply \\\\(p_i\\\\ge\\\\ell_i\\\\). Translating by \\\\(\\\\ell\\\\) therefore identifies \\\\(F\\\\) with\n\n\\\\[\nK=\\\\{x\\\\in\\\\mathbb R\"'^3:0'\"\\\\le x_i\\\\le w_i,\\\\quad x_A+x_B+x_C=r\\\\}.\n\\\\]\n\nTranslation preserves centroids, and the Vickrey-nearest point becomes the point of \\\\(K\\\\) minimizing \\\\(\\\\sum_i x_i\"'^2'\"\\\\). Here all \\\\(w_i>0\\\\) and \\\\(0<r<\\\\sum_iw_i\\\\), so \\\\(K\\\\) has positive area in its plane.\n\n**A centroid fact.** For positive caps \\\\(w_i\\\\) and \\\\(0<r<\\\\sum_iw_i\\\\), the centroid \\\\(c\\\\) of \\\\(K\\\\) has equal coordinates if and only if\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{\\\\quad r\\\\le\\\\min_i w_i\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad w_A=w_B=w_C.\\\\quad} \\\\tag{1}\n\\\\]\n\nSufficiency follows from symmetry: in the first case all upper bounds are redundant, giving an equilateral triangle; in the second case \\\\(K\\\\) is invariant under every coordinate permutation.\n\nFor necessity, suppose \\\\(w_i<w_j\\\\). Slice \\\\(K\\\\) at a fixed value of the remaining coordinate \\\\(x_k=t\\\\), and put \\\\(s=r-t\\\\). The feasible interval for \\\\(x_i\\\\) is\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\max(0,s-w_j)\\\\le x_i\\\\le\\\\min(w_i,s).\n\\\\]\n\nThe mean of \\\\(x_j-x_i\\\\) along this segment is\n\n\\\\[\ns-\\\\max(0,s-w_j)-\\\\min(w_i,s)\n=\\\\min(s,w_j)-\\\\min(s,w_i)\\\\ge0.\n\\\\]\n\nIf \\\\(r>w_i\\\\), it is strictly positive on a positive-area collection of slices. Indeed, choose\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\max(0,r-w_i-w_j)<t<\\\\min(w_k,r-w_i).\n\\\\]\n\nThis interval is nonempty because \\\\(r>w_i\\\\) and \\\\(r<\\\\sum_iw_i\\\\). These slices have positive length and \\\\(s>w_i\\\\). Integrating the slice means, weighted by their lengths, gives \\\\(c_j>c_i\\\\). Thus, if the caps are unequal and \\\\(r>\\\\min_iw_i\\\\), choose a smallest cap and a larger cap to see that the centroid coordinates cannot all be equal. This proves (1).\n\n**When the centroid minimizes distance.** The centroid is in the relative interior of \\\\(K\\\\). If it minimizes \\\\(\\\\sum_i x_i\"'^2'\"\\\\), differentiation along each direction \\\\(e_i-e_j\\\\) gives \\\\(c_i=c_j\\\\). Conversely, if its coordinates are equal, it is \\\\((r/3,r/3,r/3)\\\\), the unique minimum of that squared norm on the entire plane \\\\(\\\\sum_i x_i=r\\\\). Hence (1) is precisely the condition for the two points to agree. When they agree, their payment vector is\n\n\\\\[\np_i=\\\\ell_i+\\\\frac r3.\n\\\\]\n\n**Translate the condition back to values.** Sort the winner values as \\\\(a\\\\le b\\\\le c\\\\). Their effective caps are ordered in the same way. Since \\\\(r=\\\\sum_iw_i-d\\\\),\n\n\\\\[\nr\\\\le\\\\min_iw_i\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\n\\\\min(b,d)+\\\\min(c,d)\\\\le d\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\nb+c\\\\le d\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\nv_P\\\\le a.\n\\\\]\n\nFor the middle equivalence, if \\\\(c\\\\ge d\\\\), the left side is strictly greater than \\\\(d\\\\) because \\\\(b>0\\\\); otherwise both caps equal their original values. Also,\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\min(a,d)=\\\\min(b,d)=\\\\min(c,d)\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\nd\\\\le a\\\\ \\\\text{ or }\\\\ a=b=c.\n\\\\]\n\nThe condition \\\\(d\\\\le a\\\\) is exactly \\\\(v_P\\\\ge b+c=V-m\\\\). These are the three alternatives in the theorem.\n\nFinally, \\\\(v_P=0\\\\) gives \\\\(F=\\\\{0\\\\}\\\\). If a winner value is zero and \\\\(v_P>0\\\\), omit its fixed zero coordinate. In the same translation, at most two caps \\\\(w_1,w_2\\\\le d\\\\) remain (padding with a zero cap if necessary), and \\\\(r=w_1+w_2-d\\\\). Thus \\\\(r\\\\le w_1,w_2\\\\), so all upper bounds are redundant: the translated face is the segment from \\\\((r,0)\\\\) to \\\\((0,r)\\\\), or a point if \\\\(r=0\\\\). Its minimum-norm point is its midpoint, proving the zero-value assertion.\n\n## Worked example where they differ\n\nTake\n\n\\\\[\n(v_P,v_A,v_B,v_C)=(2,1,2,2).\n\\\\]\n\nHere \\\\(V=5\\\\), \\\\(m=1\\\\), and neither \\\\(2\\\\le1\\\\) nor \\\\(2\\\\ge4\\\\) holds; the winner values are unequal. VCG charges zero to every winner, and\n\n\\\\[\nF=\\\\{p_A+p_B+p_C=2,\\\\quad 0\\\\le p_A\\\\le1,\\\\quad p_B,p_C\\\\ge0\\\\}.\n\\\\]\n\nThe caps \\\\(p_B,p_C\\\\le2\\\\) follow from these constraints. The equal-payment point is feasible and minimizes the squared distance to zero, so\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{p\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VN}}=(2/3,2/3,2/3).}\n\\\\]\n\nTo compute the area centroid, start with the triangle having vertices \\\\((2,0,0),(0,2,0),(0,0,2)\\\\), whose centroid is \\\\((2/3,2/3,2/3)\\\\). Remove the triangle having vertices \\\\((2,0,0),(1,1,0),(1,0,1)\\\\), whose centroid is \\\\((4/3,1/3,1/3)\\\\). The removed triangle has one quarter of the original area. Therefore\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\operatorname{centroid}(F)\n=\\\\frac{4(2/3,2/3,2/3)-(4/3,1/3,1/3)}3\n=\\\\boxed{(4/9,7/9,7/9)},\n\\\\]\n\nwhich differs from the Vickrey-nearest point.\nEOF\nhead -n 5 /work/answer.md\ncat /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > /work/answer.md <<'EOF'\n# Exact condition\n\nWrite \\\\(V=v_A+v_B+v_C\\\\) and \\\\(m=\\\\min(v_A,v_B,v_C)\\\\), with the usual nonnegative valuations and \\\\(v_P<V\\\\). If all three winner values are positive, then\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{\\\\quad p\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VN}}=\\\\operatorname{centroid}(F)\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\nv_P\\\\le m\\\\ \\\\text{ or }\\\\ v_P\\\\ge V-m\\\\ \\\\text{ or }\\\\ v_A=v_B=v_C.\\\\quad}\n\\\\]\n\nThus, if the winner values are unequal, equality holds exactly when the package value is at most the smallest winner value, or at least the sum of the two largest winner values. The endpoints are included.\n\nIf any winner value is zero, equality always holds: the face is a segment or a point, and the Vickrey-nearest point is its centroid. Here and below, a lower-dimensional face has its centroid with respect to its own dimension.\n\n## Proof\n\nFirst suppose all winner values and \\\\(v_P\\\\) are positive. Set\n\n\\\\[\nd=V-v_P>0,\\\\qquad\n\\\\ell_i=(v_i-d)_+=p_i\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VCG}},\\\\qquad\nw_i=v_i-\\\\ell_i=\\\\min(v_i,d),\\\\qquad\nr=v_P-\\\\sum_i\\\\ell_i=\\\\sum_iw_i-d.\n\\\\]\n\n**Reduction to a capped simplex.** The core constraint for all three winners requires total payments at least \\\\(v_P\\\\). Conversely, every vector satisfying\n\n\\\\[\n0\\\\le p_i\\\\le v_i,\\\\qquad \\\\sum_i p_i=v_P\n\\\\]\n\nis in the core: for any subset \\\\(T\\\\),\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\sum_{i\\\\in T}p_i\n=v_P-\\\\sum_{j\\\\notin T}p_j\n\\\\ge v_P-\\\\sum_{j\\\\notin T}v_j,\n\\\\]\n\nand its payments are nonnegative. Such vectors exist because \\\\(0<v_P<V\\\\). Consequently, the minimum revenue is \\\\(v_P\\\\), and these vectors constitute \\\\(F\\\\).\n\nThe singleton constraints imply \\\\(p_i\\\\ge\\\\ell_i\\\\). Translating by \\\\(\\\\ell\\\\) therefore identifies \\\\(F\\\\) with\n\n\\\\[\nK=\\\\{x\\\\in\\\\mathbb R\"'^3:0'\"\\\\le x_i\\\\le w_i,\\\\quad x_A+x_B+x_C=r\\\\}.\n\\\\]\n\nTranslation preserves centroids, and the Vickrey-nearest point becomes the point of \\\\(K\\\\) minimizing \\\\(\\\\sum_i x_i\"'^2'\"\\\\). Here all \\\\(w_i>0\\\\) and \\\\(0<r<\\\\sum_iw_i\\\\), so \\\\(K\\\\) has positive area in its plane.\n\n**A centroid fact.** For positive caps \\\\(w_i\\\\) and \\\\(0<r<\\\\sum_iw_i\\\\), the centroid \\\\(c\\\\) of \\\\(K\\\\) has equal coordinates if and only if\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{\\\\quad r\\\\le\\\\min_i w_i\\\\quad\\\\text{or}\\\\quad w_A=w_B=w_C.\\\\quad} \\\\tag{1}\n\\\\]\n\nSufficiency follows from symmetry: in the first case all upper bounds are redundant, giving an equilateral triangle; in the second case \\\\(K\\\\) is invariant under every coordinate permutation.\n\nFor necessity, suppose \\\\(w_i<w_j\\\\). Slice \\\\(K\\\\) at a fixed value of the remaining coordinate \\\\(x_k=t\\\\), and put \\\\(s=r-t\\\\). The feasible interval for \\\\(x_i\\\\) is\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\max(0,s-w_j)\\\\le x_i\\\\le\\\\min(w_i,s).\n\\\\]\n\nThe mean of \\\\(x_j-x_i\\\\) along this segment is\n\n\\\\[\ns-\\\\max(0,s-w_j)-\\\\min(w_i,s)\n=\\\\min(s,w_j)-\\\\min(s,w_i)\\\\ge0.\n\\\\]\n\nIf \\\\(r>w_i\\\\), it is strictly positive on a positive-area collection of slices. Indeed, choose\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\max(0,r-w_i-w_j)<t<\\\\min(w_k,r-w_i).\n\\\\]\n\nThis interval is nonempty because \\\\(r>w_i\\\\) and \\\\(r<\\\\sum_iw_i\\\\). These slices have positive length and \\\\(s>w_i\\\\). Integrating the slice means, weighted by their lengths, gives \\\\(c_j>c_i\\\\). Thus, if the caps are unequal and \\\\(r>\\\\min_iw_i\\\\), choose a smallest cap and a larger cap to see that the centroid coordinates cannot all be equal. This proves (1).\n\n**When the centroid minimizes distance.** The centroid is in the relative interior of \\\\(K\\\\). If it minimizes \\\\(\\\\sum_i x_i\"'^2'\"\\\\), differentiation along each direction \\\\(e_i-e_j\\\\) gives \\\\(c_i=c_j\\\\). Conversely, if its coordinates are equal, it is \\\\((r/3,r/3,r/3)\\\\), the unique minimum of that squared norm on the entire plane \\\\(\\\\sum_i x_i=r\\\\). Hence (1) is precisely the condition for the two points to agree. When they agree, their payment vector is\n\n\\\\[\np_i=\\\\ell_i+\\\\frac r3.\n\\\\]\n\n**Translate the condition back to values.** Sort the winner values as \\\\(a\\\\le b\\\\le c\\\\). Their effective caps are ordered in the same way. Since \\\\(r=\\\\sum_iw_i-d\\\\),\n\n\\\\[\nr\\\\le\\\\min_iw_i\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\n\\\\min(b,d)+\\\\min(c,d)\\\\le d\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\nb+c\\\\le d\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\nv_P\\\\le a.\n\\\\]\n\nFor the middle equivalence, if \\\\(c\\\\ge d\\\\), the left side is strictly greater than \\\\(d\\\\) because \\\\(b>0\\\\); otherwise both caps equal their original values. Also,\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\min(a,d)=\\\\min(b,d)=\\\\min(c,d)\n\\\\quad\\\\Longleftrightarrow\\\\quad\nd\\\\le a\\\\ \\\\text{ or }\\\\ a=b=c.\n\\\\]\n\nThe condition \\\\(d\\\\le a\\\\) is exactly \\\\(v_P\\\\ge b+c=V-m\\\\). These are the three alternatives in the theorem.\n\nFinally, \\\\(v_P=0\\\\) gives \\\\(F=\\\\{0\\\\}\\\\). If a winner value is zero and \\\\(v_P>0\\\\), omit its fixed zero coordinate. In the same translation, at most two caps \\\\(w_1,w_2\\\\le d\\\\) remain (padding with a zero cap if necessary), and \\\\(r=w_1+w_2-d\\\\). Thus \\\\(r\\\\le w_1,w_2\\\\), so all upper bounds are redundant: the translated face is the segment from \\\\((r,0)\\\\) to \\\\((0,r)\\\\), or a point if \\\\(r=0\\\\). Its minimum-norm point is its midpoint, proving the zero-value assertion.\n\n## Worked example where they differ\n\nTake\n\n\\\\[\n(v_P,v_A,v_B,v_C)=(2,1,2,2).\n\\\\]\n\nHere \\\\(V=5\\\\), \\\\(m=1\\\\), and neither \\\\(2\\\\le1\\\\) nor \\\\(2\\\\ge4\\\\) holds; the winner values are unequal. VCG charges zero to every winner, and\n\n\\\\[\nF=\\\\{p_A+p_B+p_C=2,\\\\quad 0\\\\le p_A\\\\le1,\\\\quad p_B,p_C\\\\ge0\\\\}.\n\\\\]\n\nThe caps \\\\(p_B,p_C\\\\le2\\\\) follow from these constraints. The equal-payment point is feasible and minimizes the squared distance to zero, so\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\boxed{p\"'^{'\"\\\\mathrm{VN}}=(2/3,2/3,2/3).}\n\\\\]\n\nTo compute the area centroid, start with the triangle having vertices \\\\((2,0,0),(0,2,0),(0,0,2)\\\\), whose centroid is \\\\((2/3,2/3,2/3)\\\\). Remove the triangle having vertices \\\\((2,0,0),(1,1,0),(1,0,1)\\\\), whose centroid is \\\\((4/3,1/3,1/3)\\\\). The removed triangle has one quarter of the original area. Therefore\n\n\\\\[\n\\\\operatorname{centroid}(F)\n=\\\\frac{4(2/3,2/3,2/3)-(4/3,1/3,1/3)}3\n=\\\\boxed{(4/9,7/9,7/9)},\n\\\\]\n\nwhich differs from the Vickrey-nearest point.\nEOF\nhead -n 5 /work/answer.md\ncat /work/board.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Exact condition\n\nWrite \\(V=v_A+v_B+v_C\\) and \\(m=\\min(v_A,v_B,v_C)\\), with the usual nonnegative valuations and \\(v_P<V\\). If all three winner values are positive, then\n\n\\[\n\n[sol] 2026-10-22 UTC: Reading problem; will derive geometry and exact centroid equality condition. Please coordinate independent derivation/checks on board.\n\n[sol] 2026-10-22 20:45 UTC: Derived F={0<=p_i<=v_i, sum p=P}; let d=sum v-P, l_i=max(0,v_i-d), w_i=min(v_i,d), r=sum w-d. Shift x=p-l gives capped simplex sum x=r, caps w, VN projection of 0. For all positive v and P>0, conjectured/proving exact equality iff all w equal OR r<=min w. Centroid ordering lemma: if w_i<w_j and w_i<r then centroid_i<centroid_j. VN=centroid must be interior, hence uniform x. Handling zero values/degeneracies next.\n\n[sol] 2026-10-22 20:47 UTC: Simplified positive-value condition (sort a<=b<=c): equality iff P<=a OR P>=b+c OR a=b=c. This follows r<=min w iff P<=a; all w equal iff d<=a (P>=b+c) or original a=b=c. If any winner value is zero, F has <=1 dimension and equality always (shifted cap simplex bounds redundant). P=0 singleton. Will write proof via centroid pair-ordering/reflection and include example (vA,vB,vC)=(1,2,2), P=2.\n\n[sol] 2026-10-22 20:50 UTC: Wrote complete draft answer.md with simple original-value condition, proof by slice ordering, zero-value cases, and example (2,1,2,2): VN=(2/3,2/3,2/3), centroid=(4/9,7/9,7/9). Please review/check and append agreement or corrections; I will independently numerically stress-test.\n\n[sol] 2026-10-22 20:44 UTC: Timestamp correction: earlier 20:45--20:50 board labels were estimates; actual current UTC is about 20:44. Draft complete; doing exact rational polygon checks and awaiting argon review.\n\n[sol] 2026-10-22 20:44:02 UTC: Independent exact-rational clipping/shoelace centroid + water-filling projection checks passed all 4,320 cases (winner values 1..6, package values positive half-integers below total). Proof and example checked; only waiting for argon review/agreement before final.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
